<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tutankhamun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1342–1323 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tutankhamen, Tutankhaten, King Tut · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tutankhamun was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a> of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> who ruled c. 1332–1323 BCE and died at about the age of 19. Born Tutankhaten in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, he restored the traditional religion after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a>, restoring the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, changing his name and moving the court from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> back to Memphis. DNA testing identified his father as the mummy from tomb KV55, thought to be Akhenaten or Smenkhkare. As his royal tomb was unfinished, he was buried in a small non-royal tomb, which excavators led by Howard Carter found in 1922 with much of its contents, over 5,000 artifacts and the king's undisturbed mummy; the discovery renewed public interest in ancient Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tutankhamun.jpg" alt="Tutankhamun" width="330" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MykReeve · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tutanchamun_Maske.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tutankhamun was born in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-period">Amarna Period</a>. His original name, Tutankhaten or Tutankhuaten, meant living image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>, reflecting the shift to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a> that characterised Akhenaten's reign. When he became king he married Ankhesenpaaten, one of Akhenaten's daughters, who later changed her name to Ankhesenamun. They had two daughters who died at or soon after birth and were buried with him in his tomb.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tutankhamun became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> between 8 and 9 years of age, after the short reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>'s successors Smenkhkare and Neferneferuaten, and he took the throne name Nebkheperure. At the start of his reign the court was still at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> was still acknowledged, but his court was trying to reconcile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a> with the traditional religion. Activity at Amarna decreased during the first four years, and given the king's age the reversals of Akhenaten's policies must have been instigated by his advisors. His restorations of cults were recorded on the Restoration Stela.</p><p>He sought to restore diplomatic relations with other kingdoms, in particular with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, and battles with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> and Asiatics were recorded in his mortuary temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, both as Egyptian victories. He was one of the few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> worshipped as a deity during their lifetime, as temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and stelae at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> indicate. There are no surviving records of the circumstances of his death. Hawass and his team postulate that it resulted from a leg fracture, perhaps from a fall, combined with a severe malarial infection.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tutankhamun's two known children were stillborn or died shortly after birth, and his death left no immediate heir. Ay, who performs the opening of the mouth ritual for him on the burial chamber wall, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horemheb">Horemheb</a>, commander-in-chief of his armed forces, succeeded Ay. Horemheb completed the restoration of the traditional religion and had his own name inscribed on monuments that Tutankhamun had commissioned.</p><p>His tomb was unusually small for his status, and its entrance was lost under debris from later tombs and workers' houses. Howard Carter found it in 1922, with the patronage of George Herbert, 5th Earl of Carnarvon. It is the only royal tomb in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a> found in near-intact condition, with 5,398 items, among them a solid gold coffin, a face mask, thrones, bows and trumpets, and Carter took ten years to catalogue them. Rumours of a curse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> followed the death of Carnarvon on 5 April 1923. In 1962 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> held an exhibition of the treasures that drew 1.2 million visitors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tutankhamun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…as Egypt declined: the Akkar Plain rebelled with Hittite support to become the kingdom of Amurru, the Mitanni Empire that had dominated Syria fell apart under the campaigns of Suppiluliuma I of Hatti, and <b>Tutankhamun</b> and his general Horemheb scrambled to keep Egypt's hold on the southern Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death, under the young <b>Tutankhamun</b>, the old gods were restored, and later kings erased Akhenaten from the record.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>At the British Museum, “The First Emperor: China’s Terracotta Army” ran from 2007 to 2008, brought the most visitors since the <b>Tutankhamun</b> exhibition of 1972, and sold 400,000 advance tickets so fast that the museum extended its opening hours until midnight.</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>War was the quickest way up for an able man: soldiers won rewards and land, and Horemheb, a general under <b>Tutankhamun</b>, became king himself.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>His reforms were later regarded as heretical and reversed under his successor <b>Tutankhamun</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten</a></li><li><blockquote>With Lord Carnarvon present, Howard Carter's team cleared the stairway and found a sealed doorway bearing <b>Tutankhamun</b>'s name.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-24">24 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Thebes, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.66,30.91&amp;year=-1330" aria-label="Amarna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5361 1150 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tutankhamun"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1420h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1420h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.66,30.91&amp;year=-1330">What was here in 1330 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
